{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And you shall not profane My holy name, and I will be sanctified among the children of Israel.</b> Profanation of the Name is a most severe sin. We find that the Holy One, blessed be He, pardoned idolatry but did not pardon profanation of the Name; as the prophet said (Ezekiel 20:39): ״ואתם בית ישראל איש את גלוליו לכו עבדו ואת שם קדשי לא תחללו עוד״ — 'As for you, O house of Israel, go serve your idols, but My holy name you shall no longer profane.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory said concerning this sin that there is no power in Yom ha-Kippurim to atone, nor in repentance to suspend, nor in suffering to cleanse; rather, all of them suspend and death cleanses." "<b>Now</b> we find a remedy for the sin of profanation of the Name: if one sanctifies the Name in corresponding measure to what one profaned — and therefore Scripture juxtaposed to it ונקדשתי, 'and I will be sanctified.' Thus Scripture says (Proverbs 16:6): ״בחסד ואמת יכופר עון״ — 'by kindness and truth iniquity is atoned' — that is, the iniquity of wickedness and falsehood, which are the opposite of kindness and truth, like an illness that can only be cured by its opposite. Thus our Rabbis of blessed memory explicitly said: if a person sinned with lashon ha-ra (evil speech), let him engage in Torah; if a person sinned with his eyes, let him let tears flow from them. And so David said (Psalms 119:136): ״פלגי מים ירדו עיני על לא שמרו תורתך״ — 'Rivers of water run down my eyes, because they did not keep Your Torah' — it does not say 'I did not keep' but 'they did not keep.' And further our Rabbis of blessed memory said: if you made bundles of transgressions, make against them bundles of mitzvot. Legs that hurry to run to evil — let them be runners for a matter of mitzvah; hands that shed innocent blood — let him bind tefillin on his arm and give charity to the poor." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> This is a positive commandment in the Torah: to sanctify the Name in public. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ונקדשתי בתוך בני ישראל — 'and I will be sanctified among the children of Israel' — surrender yourself to sanctify My name. One might think in private; therefore it says 'among the children of Israel.' And when one surrenders oneself, one should surrender oneself on condition to die, for whoever surrenders himself on condition of a miracle — no miracle is performed for him. Thus we find with Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, that they did not surrender themselves on condition of a miracle, as it says (Daniel 3:18): ״והן לא ידיע להוא לך מלכא די לאלהך לא איתנא פלחין ולצלם דהבא די הקימת לא נסגד, מציל ולא מציל ידיע להוא לך וגו׳״ — 'But if not, be it known to you, O king, that we will not serve your gods nor worship the golden image that you have set up; whether He delivers or does not deliver, be it known to you,' etc. And further our Rabbis of blessed memory proved from this verse that every matter of sanctity requires ten, as they said in Berakhot: there is no matter of sanctity with fewer than ten, as it says ונקדשתי בתוך בני ישראל, and it is written there (Numbers 16:21): הבדלו מתוך העדה, 'separate yourselves from the midst of the congregation' — just as there, ten, so too here, ten. And R. Jacob explained that this formulation is not precise, for one cannot bring proof from the ten spies for a matter of sanctity; but the primary formulation is thus: it is derived תוך תוך — 'midst, midst': it is written here ונקדשתי בתוך, and it is written there (Genesis 42:5): לשבור בתוך הבאים, 'to buy grain among those who came' — just as there, ten, so too here, ten. And now one brings proof from the ten brothers of Joseph, who were righteous, for a matter of sanctity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The reference to 'the ten spies' (meraglim) appears to be a rejected version of the derivation that R. Jacob critiques; the accepted version (gezeirah shavah of תוך/תוך from Genesis 42:5, yielding the ten brothers of Joseph) follows. The passage preserves both the rejected and accepted lines of reasoning as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:52.644905490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}